This is the Chrome Manifest V3 target generated by PolyPlugin Forge for `{{pluginId}}`
(`{{packageName}}`). It contains a real popup, a module background service
worker, deterministic tests, and no runtime or development dependencies. ##
Verify locally Node.js 22 or newer is required. ```bash npm ci npm test npm run
build ``` `npm ci` is fully offline once Node and npm are available because this
template has no package dependencies. The build copies the three audited
JavaScript modules into `dist/`. ## Generate and load the extension 1. Generate
`manifest.json` from `plugin.yaml` using PolyPlugin Forge. 2. Run `npm run
build`. 3. Open `chrome://extensions`, enable **Developer mode**, and choose
**Load unpacked**. 4. Select this project directory and open the
{{displayName}}
toolbar action. The generated manifest points its module service worker at
`dist/index.js` and its action popup at `popup.html`. Do not hand-edit generated
manifest fields; change `plugin.yaml` and regenerate instead. ## Behavior -
Choosing **Say hello** or pressing Enter sends a typed message to the background
service worker. - The service worker creates the greeting with the pure function
in `src/greeting.js`. - The last normalized name is kept in
`chrome.storage.local`. - Popup output uses `textContent` and an `aria-live`
status region. ## Privacy There is no telemetry or network access. The only
retained value is the last entered name, stored locally inside Chrome extension
storage. See [docs/PRIVACY.md](docs/PRIVACY.md). ## Project layout -
`plugin.yaml` — canonical PolyPlugin Forge definition. - `src/index.js` — MV3
background service worker. - `src/popup.js` — popup interaction and Chrome
messaging. - `src/greeting.js` — pure greeting domain function. - `popup.html` /
`popup.css` — accessible popup UI. - `tests/` — dependency-free `node:test`
coverage. - `scripts/build.mjs` — deterministic copy build. ## License MIT